# GIT HANDS-ON

#GIT-1: Creating and Working with a Git Repository

### Command

`git init`

**Description:**
Initializes a Git repository inside the current directory.

---

### Command

`git status`

**Description:**
Displays the current state of the repository, including tracked, untracked, and modified files.

---

### Command

`git add .`

**Description:**
Adds all the current changes and files to the staging area.

---

### Command

`git commit -m "Initial Commit"`

**Description:**
Creates a commit containing the staged files with the given commit message.

---

### Command

`git log`

**Description:**
Shows the detailed history of commits made in the repository.

---

### Command

`git log --oneline`

**Description:**
Shows the commit history in a shorter, one-line format.

---

# GIT-2: Git User Configuration

### Command

`git config --global user.name "Your Name"`

**Description:**
Configures the username that Git will associate with commits.

---

### Command

`git config --global user.email "yourmail@gmail.com"`

**Description:**
Configures the email address associated with Git commits.

---

### Command

`git config --list`

**Description:**
Lists the Git configuration settings currently available.

---

### Command

`git --version`

**Description:**
Displays the version of Git installed on the system.

---

# GIT-3: Working with Branches

### Command

`git branch`

**Description:**
Displays the branches available in the current repository.

---

### Command

`git branch feature`

**Description:**
Creates a new branch with the name `feature`.

---

### Command

`git checkout feature`

**Description:**
Moves from the current branch to the `feature` branch.

---

### Command

`git checkout -b demo`

**Description:**
Creates a branch called `demo` and immediately switches to it.

---

### Command

`git checkout main`

**Description:**
Switches back to the `main` branch.

---

### Command

`git merge feature`

**Description:**
Combines the changes from the `feature` branch into the branch that is currently active.

---

### Command

`git branch -d feature`

**Description:**
Deletes the `feature` branch after it is no longer required.

---

# GIT-4: Working with Remote Repositories

### Command

`git remote -v`

**Description:**
Displays the remote repositories and their associated URLs.

---

### Command

`git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git`

**Description:**
Connects the local Git repository to the specified GitHub repository using the remote name `origin`.

---

### Command

`git push -u origin main`

**Description:**
Pushes the local `main` branch to the remote GitHub repository and sets it as the upstream branch.

---

### Command

`git pull origin main`

**Description:**
Retrieves the latest changes from the remote `main` branch and integrates them into the local repository.

---

### Command

`git fetch`

**Description:**
Retrieves updates from the remote repository without automatically merging them into the current branch.

---

### Command

`git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git`

**Description:**
Creates a local copy of an existing remote GitHub repository.

---

# GIT-5: Reverting and Managing Changes

### Command

`git diff`

**Description:**
Displays the differences between the current changes and the existing version of files.

---

### Command

`git restore filename`

**Description:**
Discards changes made to the specified file and restores its previous version.

---

### Command

`git restore --staged filename`

**Description:**
Removes the specified file from the staging area while keeping its modifications.

---

### Command

`git reset --soft HEAD~1`

**Description:**
Removes the most recent commit while preserving the changes.

---

### Command

`git reset --hard HEAD~1`

**Description:**
Removes the latest commit along with the changes associated with it.

---

### Command

`git rm filename`

**Description:**
Removes the specified file from the Git repository.

---

### Command

`git mv oldname.txt newname.txt`

**Description:**
Renames or moves a file within the Git repository.

---

# FREQUENTLY USED GIT COMMANDS

`git init`
Creates a new Git repository.

`git status`
Checks the current repository status.

`git add .`
Stages all changes.

`git commit -m "message"`
Saves the staged changes as a new commit.

`git log`
Displays the complete commit history.

`git log --oneline`
Displays a compact version of the commit history.

`git branch`
Lists the available branches.

`git checkout branch_name`
Switches to the specified branch.

`git merge branch_name`
Merges another branch into the current branch.

`git remote -v`
Displays configured remote repositories.

`git push origin main`
Pushes the local `main` branch to the remote repository.

`git pull origin main`
Retrieves and merges the latest changes from the remote `main` branch.

`git clone repository_url`
Creates a local copy of a remote repository.

`git diff`
Shows differences between file versions.

`git restore filename`
Discards modifications made to a file.

`git reset --soft HEAD~1`
Removes the latest commit while retaining the changes.

`git reset --hard HEAD~1`
Removes the latest commit along with its changes.

