# SAMPLE GIT WORKFLOW

The following sequence demonstrates a complete Git workflow:

`mkdir GitAssignment`

Creates a new directory named `GitAssignment`.

`cd GitAssignment`

Moves into the newly created directory.

`git init`

Initializes Git in the directory.

`notepad file1.txt`

Creates or opens `file1.txt` using Notepad.

`git status`

Checks the current repository status.

`git add .`

Stages the files for committing.

`git commit -m "Created first file"`

Creates a commit for the first file.

`git branch feature`

Creates a new branch named `feature`.

`git checkout feature`

Switches to the newly created `feature` branch.

`notepad file2.txt`

Creates or opens another file named `file2.txt`.

`git add .`

Stages the newly created file.

`git commit -m "Added second file"`

Commits the second file.

`git checkout main`

Returns to the `main` branch.

`git merge feature`

Merges the work completed in the `feature` branch into `main`.

`git log --oneline`

Displays the commit history in compact form.

`git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git`

Links the local repository with the GitHub repository.

`git push -u origin main`

Uploads the local `main` branch to GitHub.

